  "query_preview": "..\n(This evening through next Monday)\n\nWEATHER PATTERN OVERVIEW: \n\nUpper level ridge of high pressure has finally started it \neastward slide as an approaching upper level trough digs across \nthe Pacific NW into California. In between these two features \nsoutherly flow will continue to bring monsoonal moisture over the \nsilver state. Tuesday afternoon will have a 20% to 30% chance for \nthunderstorms mainly along the US 50 corridor. These storms will \nagain be slow moving, allowing longer residence time over a given \narea, meaning higher chances for rain to reach the ground given \nthe dry surface layer as wet storms may produce up to 0.25\u201d or \nmore of rain. Still there will be a few dry thunderstorms that do \nform especially as storms first form each afternoon, with an \nelevated risk for ground lightning strikes causing fire starts. \nWednesday through Friday, the upper level trough will move through\nNevada, Models show that this system will help Nevada out in \nmultiple ways, first dropping temperatures significantly through \nthe end of the week with highs returning to the low 80s to low \n90s. Second, it will help channel monsoonal moisture flow \nincreasing chances for wetter storm modes. Finally, the added \ndynamic cooling and dynamic lift will also help to increase \noverall precipitation chances. The result will be a 30% to 80% \nchance for isolated to scattered afternoon and evening \nthunderstorms and showers. The highest probability will remain \nacross central Nevada, but chances have risen to 40% to 60% of \nthunderstorms for northern Nevada. Models are now showing better \nchances for all of northern Nevada to see precipitation this week.\nThere still is a possibility that NW Humboldt county gets missed,\nbut model consensus QPF now shows up to 0.25\u201d of rainfall through\nSaturday morning with ares of central NV could receive up to \n0.75\u201d or more. After Saturday the first upper trough lifts NE into\nCanada which may lower storm chances a bit over the weekend, but \nstorm chances will average 20% to 40%. Next Monday through \nWednesday Models have been inconsistent with the evolution of the \nupper pattern, and today\u2019s solutions are no different. GFS and the\nECMWF ensembles have been trading solutions of either another \nupper trough somewhere over the western US, or the return of the \nupper level ridge to the four corners or great basin. The one new \nagreement for the most recent runs is keeping the southerly \nmonsoonal flow. However, given the particular placement of either \nridge or trough will dictate how high or low storm and shower \nchances become for the longer term. So for now will hold over a \nlow 20% to 30% chance for isolated thunderstorms mainly fir \ncentral and eastern NV for the start of next week. \n\nFORECAST CONFIDENCE & DEVIATIONS: \n \nThere is high confidence for chances of isolated to scattered wet\nwith a few dry thunderstorms Through Saturday afternoon for \ncentral Nevada. \n\nThere moderate confidence for isolated to scattered mix of wet \nand dry thunderstorms expanding to cover Humboldt and Elko \ncounties Wednesday through Saturday. \n\nThere is low confidence in the evolution of the upper pattern \nafter Sunday as models are disagreement as to the strength and \nlocation of key upper level features.",
